1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,471
A circled take with 13 seconds of soft focus.

2
00:00:03,471 --> 00:00:05,400
Found in 65 milliseconds.

3
00:00:07,700 --> 00:00:13,244
One a.m. On every film set in the world, three people

4
00:00:13,244 --> 00:00:18,161
are still awake, typing up what happened today.

5
00:00:18,161 --> 00:00:23,182
Which takes got circled. How many pages we made.

6
00:00:23,482 --> 00:00:28,128
That's three crew-hours a day. Ninety hours over a feature.

7
00:00:28,128 --> 00:00:31,434
And the knowledge ends up trapped in a PDF

8
00:00:31,434 --> 00:00:34,269
that can't answer a single question.

9
00:00:34,269 --> 00:00:36,474
So we built the other thing.

10
00:00:36,774 --> 00:00:41,564
Gemini three point five Flash watches every take.

11
00:00:41,564 --> 00:00:46,549
Dialogue, boom in shot, soft focus, a flubbed line.

12
00:00:46,549 --> 00:00:52,219
And we measure the file itself twenty-five times a second:

13
00:00:52,219 --> 00:00:57,400
focus, exposure, audio peak. Both land in ClickHouse.

14
00:00:57,400 --> 00:01:00,822
Three million rows, one production.

15
00:01:01,122 --> 00:01:04,396
Now ask something nobody could ask before.

16
00:01:04,396 --> 00:01:07,826
The director circled scene twelve, take two.

17
00:01:07,826 --> 00:01:12,036
It is soft for thirteen seconds, and nobody caught it.

18
00:01:12,036 --> 00:01:14,843
That answer joins what Gemini saw to

19
00:01:14,843 --> 00:01:17,493
what the camera actually measured.

20
00:01:17,493 --> 00:01:21,391
Three million rows, sixty-five milliseconds, every

21
00:01:21,391 --> 00:01:25,211
query through the official ClickHouse MCP server.

22
00:01:25,211 --> 00:01:28,642
That's a reshoot you don't have to schedule.

23
00:01:28,942 --> 00:01:31,004
Ask like an assistant editor:

24
00:01:31,004 --> 00:01:35,128
the line, the take, the timecode, and it plays from there.

25
00:01:35,128 --> 00:01:36,550
Ask like a producer:

26
00:01:36,550 --> 00:01:40,461
forty-eight and four-eighths pages of fifty-two — three

27
00:01:40,461 --> 00:01:44,230
and a half pages behind after day twelve, yet on pace

28
00:01:44,230 --> 00:01:47,288
to finish with a day and a half of cushion.

29
00:01:47,288 --> 00:01:50,914
It knows days eight and eleven lost setups to rain.

30
00:01:51,214 --> 00:01:53,466
Ask like a script supervisor.

31
00:01:53,466 --> 00:01:56,649
It reads across every take of a scene and

32
00:01:56,649 --> 00:01:59,677
tells you which one is the odd one out.

33
00:01:59,977 --> 00:02:03,098
And at wrap it writes the paperwork.

34
00:02:03,098 --> 00:02:06,393
The progress report, the editor's log,

35
00:02:06,393 --> 00:02:09,254
the facing pages in digital form.

36
00:02:09,254 --> 00:02:13,415
Pages in eighths, setups, circled, NG, overtime.

37
00:02:13,415 --> 00:02:17,230
Every field is a live query, not a template.

38
00:02:17,230 --> 00:02:21,044
And it reads you the short version out loud.

39
00:02:21,344 --> 00:02:24,448
Same database, straight through Grafana.

40
00:02:24,448 --> 00:02:27,163
Print ratio by scene, pages against

41
00:02:27,163 --> 00:02:29,801
plan, where the flags are landing.

42
00:02:29,801 --> 00:02:32,826
And every take in the shoot, browsable.

43
00:02:33,126 --> 00:02:37,240
Gemini and Google Cloud Agent Builder on Cloud Run.

44
00:02:37,240 --> 00:02:40,951
ClickHouse as the production's memory, reached

45
00:02:40,951 --> 00:02:43,935
only through its official MCP server.

46
00:02:43,935 --> 00:02:48,533
It's live, it's open source, and the link is right there.

47
00:02:48,833 --> 00:02:53,221
SlateIQ. Your dailies, finally talking back.
